[{:db/id 17592186054634 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1f4365e0-4fb2-5b82-b78f-2c814bc881e5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054635 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8aca702c-860b-5bdc-89e7-b6ee88897db2" "question-619a2767-aaa5-5efa-bd74-7ab475387f3d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c44203d-f0c3-504e-a004-3addf0955223"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054995 :question/problem "For the sets $Z={-1,0,1}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(-1,0,1)\\in Y$\n- $(-1,1)\\in Y$\n- $(0,0)\\in Y$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cb962e74701db361807c237a521f13a1c753e4acc6d68a1cfb1694bdfbd45bc.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-1,-1),(-1,0),(-1,1),(0,-1),(0,0),(0,1),(1,-1),(1,0),(1,1)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186051500 :answer-field/correct 17592186051502 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f4365e0-4fb2-5b82-b78f-2c814bc881e5" :question/id #uuid "1f4365e0-4fb2-5b82-b78f-2c814bc881e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-182014" :question/problem "For the sets $A={0,1,2},$ $B={4,6,8},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(0,2)\\in C$\n- $(1,8)\\in C$\n- $(0,6)\\in C$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/12/12d1b94cc6d53954a9c164bd069f77fcddc7a46de2c1701c1f4cdfc608eef185.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(0,4),(0,6),(0,8),(1,4),(1,6),(1,8),(2,4),(2,6),(2,8)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6a82ca7-01c6-5a96-826e-af1700b4e1e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6a82ca7-01c6-5a96-826e-af1700b4e1e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a82ca7-01c6-5a96-826e-af1700b4e1e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c5a110c3-4357-5dd4-8b2e-d63c84712e94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f94df467-1917-54c6-9462-f00a0bb4ad5a" "answer-117c917b-7d62-5b51-804f-8f0f908de52c" "answer-8a3b603c-2196-5e99-b012-5950a3d2c4b3" "answer-c5a110c3-4357-5dd4-8b2e-d63c84712e94" "answer-24030462-28a2-5ff6-ab9c-6ac8a212b6d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f94df467-1917-54c6-9462-f00a0bb4ad5a" :answer/id #uuid "f94df467-1917-54c6-9462-f00a0bb4ad5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-117c917b-7d62-5b51-804f-8f0f908de52c" :answer/id #uuid "117c917b-7d62-5b51-804f-8f0f908de52c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a3b603c-2196-5e99-b012-5950a3d2c4b3" :answer/id #uuid "8a3b603c-2196-5e99-b012-5950a3d2c4b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5a110c3-4357-5dd4-8b2e-d63c84712e94" :answer/id #uuid "c5a110c3-4357-5dd4-8b2e-d63c84712e94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24030462-28a2-5ff6-ab9c-6ac8a212b6d5" :answer/id #uuid "24030462-28a2-5ff6-ab9c-6ac8a212b6d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8aca702c-860b-5bdc-89e7-b6ee88897db2" :question/id #uuid "8aca702c-860b-5bdc-89e7-b6ee88897db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80443" :question/problem "What is the cardinality of $B\\times A$ for $B={1,2,3,...,100}$ and $A={a}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any two finite sets $B$ and $A,$ the cardinality of $B\\times A$ is\n\n$$\n|B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |A|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B\\times A| & =|B|\\cdot |A| \\\\  & =100\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98df9e75-9a1d-5fb1-8cca-cc1e11240df1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98df9e75-9a1d-5fb1-8cca-cc1e11240df1" :answer-field/id #uuid "98df9e75-9a1d-5fb1-8cca-cc1e11240df1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c392020-591e-554e-9445-e43db05bc4ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c392020-591e-554e-9445-e43db05bc4ea" "answer-9f0a0c9c-17ab-5998-a126-74c2690405d7" "answer-a92c961b-342f-5255-926d-25df1da4fb4c" "answer-5427fa39-7620-5a39-b0e5-443eefe2442e" "answer-4248df2e-bd43-540d-b809-a47cb096e53f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c392020-591e-554e-9445-e43db05bc4ea" :answer/id #uuid "6c392020-591e-554e-9445-e43db05bc4ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f0a0c9c-17ab-5998-a126-74c2690405d7" :answer/id #uuid "9f0a0c9c-17ab-5998-a126-74c2690405d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "101" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a92c961b-342f-5255-926d-25df1da4fb4c" :answer/id #uuid "a92c961b-342f-5255-926d-25df1da4fb4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5427fa39-7620-5a39-b0e5-443eefe2442e" :answer/id #uuid "5427fa39-7620-5a39-b0e5-443eefe2442e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4248df2e-bd43-540d-b809-a47cb096e53f" :answer/id #uuid "4248df2e-bd43-540d-b809-a47cb096e53f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-619a2767-aaa5-5efa-bd74-7ab475387f3d" :question/id #uuid "619a2767-aaa5-5efa-bd74-7ab475387f3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80895" :question/problem "What is the cardinality of $Z\\times X$ for $Z={p,q,r}$ and $X={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any two finite sets $Z$ and $X,$ the cardinality of $Z\\times X$ is\n\n$$\n|Z\\times X|=|Z|\\cdot |X|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|Z\\times X| & =|Z|\\cdot |X| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b1f4f72-6f18-5497-b49c-9ae7df83c964"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b1f4f72-6f18-5497-b49c-9ae7df83c964" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1f4f72-6f18-5497-b49c-9ae7df83c964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bcc3958-b0e2-5ab6-aeba-37976f8695e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e45089dc-a2a0-5a75-a15b-c75c064e9ae2" "answer-63a9f909-0100-5b97-9f7a-618d75a1ed03" "answer-3bcc3958-b0e2-5ab6-aeba-37976f8695e3" "answer-5971eb2f-7b93-5176-8493-6729a333bd1c" "answer-46d5e59a-a604-5e1d-a944-0b0db4cf9c5a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e45089dc-a2a0-5a75-a15b-c75c064e9ae2" :answer/id #uuid "e45089dc-a2a0-5a75-a15b-c75c064e9ae2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63a9f909-0100-5b97-9f7a-618d75a1ed03" :answer/id #uuid "63a9f909-0100-5b97-9f7a-618d75a1ed03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bcc3958-b0e2-5ab6-aeba-37976f8695e3" :answer/id #uuid "3bcc3958-b0e2-5ab6-aeba-37976f8695e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5971eb2f-7b93-5176-8493-6729a333bd1c" :answer/id #uuid "5971eb2f-7b93-5176-8493-6729a333bd1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46d5e59a-a604-5e1d-a944-0b0db4cf9c5a" :answer/id #uuid "46d5e59a-a604-5e1d-a944-0b0db4cf9c5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c44203d-f0c3-504e-a004-3addf0955223" :question/id #uuid "1c44203d-f0c3-504e-a004-3addf0955223" :question/math-academy-id "q-181956" :question/problem "If $A={-2,2}$ and $B={c,d},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times B?$\n\n- $(-2,d,c)$\n- $(-2,(c,d))$\n- $((2,d),c)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${-2,2}$ with ${c,d},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/98/98e7cfca515290b952ea5aefc9b2516a5cd6a68de0dc048c2075128e6c60b651.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(-2,c),(-2,d),(2,c),(2,d)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e96aeb1c5bfb1f9faa590a8eb8b01528a1ce9431a3f1ceac92b156a9bdde221.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times B|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |B|=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((2,d),c)\\in (A\\times B)\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f342874d-f251-593c-aa5a-839fe9ae8390"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f342874d-f251-593c-aa5a-839fe9ae8390" :answer-field/id #uuid "f342874d-f251-593c-aa5a-839fe9ae8390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f1c11f8-7017-508a-a025-032d058eefc3" "answer-df07081f-dcec-5eb4-b4c9-6e0e88404217" "answer-c6ed4afd-f54e-59d9-bd0a-604a3f0d6358" "answer-9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" "answer-323a0eaa-c97b-52de-a3bc-ebc72ae2e5d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f1c11f8-7017-508a-a025-032d058eefc3" :answer/id #uuid "3f1c11f8-7017-508a-a025-032d058eefc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df07081f-dcec-5eb4-b4c9-6e0e88404217" :answer/id #uuid "df07081f-dcec-5eb4-b4c9-6e0e88404217" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6ed4afd-f54e-59d9-bd0a-604a3f0d6358" :answer/id #uuid "c6ed4afd-f54e-59d9-bd0a-604a3f0d6358" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/id #uuid "9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-323a0eaa-c97b-52de-a3bc-ebc72ae2e5d4" :answer/id #uuid "323a0eaa-c97b-52de-a3bc-ebc72ae2e5d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056042 :tutorial/content "An **ordered pair** $(x, y)$ is a pair of mathematical objects $x$ and $y$ written in parentheses and separated by a comma. \n\nYou are already familiar with an ordered pair $(x,y)$ representing a point in the Cartesian plane. In this context, $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. However, more generally, the coordinates $x$ and $y$ of an ordered pair can be any mathematical objects we like! Moreover, they do not need to be the same type of object. \n\nHere are some examples of ordered pairs.\n\n![](images/bf/bf99b45f9e9ab363314f3604ef6316c7fe7e63623452f2fe78d597dd60c2d302.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We say that an ordered pair $(x,y)$ has **coordinates** $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Given two ordered pairs $(a, b)$ and $(c, d),$ we say they're **equal** if $a = c$ and $b = d,$ and we write \n\n$$\n(a, b) = (c, d).\n$$\n\nFor example, $(2, 3) = (2, 3)$ but $(2, 3) \\ne (3, 2).$\n\nWe use ordered pairs to define the so-called **Cartesian product** of two sets. Let's discuss this in more detail." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056052 :tutorial/content "The **Cartesian product** of two sets $A$ and $B,$ denoted $A \\times B,$ is a set containing all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$  \n\nWe define the Cartesian product using the following constructive definition:\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the following finite sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{x, y\\}, \\qquad B  = \\{1, 2, 3\\}\n$$\n\nTo help visualize the Cartesian product $A\\times B,$ we'll use a visual schematic.\n\nFirst, we create a table and list the elements of $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](images/ec/ec3087067a79d46751e7ae67d34543b0507f3425e92a50eb8fb242b35b463b8b.png)\n\nThen, at each location marked $\\ast$, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and whose second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\n![](images/f9/f91a0e2ec091f90395c59330597b55c9e55aad73ac4bc052e2b500ea9db894b2.png)\n\nTo write down the Cartesian product $A\\times B,$ we construct a set containing all ordered pairs in our schematic. This gives\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(x, 1), (x, 2), (x, 3), (y, 1), (y, 2), (y, 3)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's now consider $B\\times A.$ The schematic for this set is shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59aca653639bf7499634ade12390f0c4d0f6fb0bb3dd34359fc5a53c49da3d8.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB\\times A = \\big\\{(1, x), (1, y), (2, x), (2, y), (3, x), (3, y)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $(a,b)\\neq (b,a),$ we have that $A\\times B  \\neq B\\times A$ in general. \n\nWe can also construct the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$ The schematic for this set is given below. \n\n![](images/00/00008245f5a72373c268ebe551e707b0f981c7ee03ec3cb0351288ea9d625f03.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA\\times A = A^2 = \\big\\{(x, x), (x, y), (y, x), (y, y)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that the Cartesian product of *any* set with the empty set equals the empty set:\n\n$$\nA \\times \\emptyset = \\emptyset, \\qquad \\emptyset \\times A = \\emptyset\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054908 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
